  "worked_solution": "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L3}.$ Since $\u03a6(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=Ax(t).$ This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular, \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from the fact that ${x}_{2}\\text{\u00a0is a solution of the system}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L4}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ By definition, the derivative of $\u03a6(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column,\n\n$$\n{\u03a6}^{\u2032}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t) & {x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing ${x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ and ${x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$ from $\\text{L3},$ we substitute each derivative into the matrix to get\n\n$$\n{\u03a6}^{\u2032}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by substitution from $\\text{L2}\\text{\u00a0and\u00a0}\\text{L3}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ From $\\text{L6}$ we have\n\n$$\n{\u03a6}^{\u2032}(t)=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)].\n$$\n\nUsing the definition $\u03a6(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$ from $\\text{L1},$ we substitute to obtain\n\n$$\n{\u03a6}^{\u2032}(t)=A\u03a6(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L6}$ by substitution from $\\text{L1}.$",
